  "generated_text": "We can use the binomial theorem to simplify the expression. The binomial theorem states that:\n\n\\[\n(x + y)^n = \\sum_{k=0}^{n} \\binom{n}{k} x^{n-k} y^k\n\\]\n\nIn our case, let \\(x = 1\\) and \\(y = -1\\). Then:\n\n\\[\n(1 + (-1))^{100} = \\sum_{k=0}^{100} \\binom{100}{k} 1^{100-k} (-1)^k\n\\]\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n\\[\n0^{100} = \\sum_{k=0}^{100} \\binom{100}{k} (-1)^k\n\\]\n\nSince \\(0^{100} = 0\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n0 = \\sum_{k=0}^{100} \\binom{100}{k} (-1)^k\n\\]\n\nThis implies that the sum of the alternating binomial coefficients is zero.\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[0, '0']",
    "gold_answer": "By the Binomial Theorem,\n\\[(x + y)^{100} = \\binom{100}{0} x^{100} + \\binom{100}{1} x^{99} y + \\binom{100}{2} x^{98} y^2 + \\dots + \\binom{100}{100} y^{100}.\\]Setting $x = 1$ and $y = -1,$ we get\n\\[\\binom{100}{0} - \\binom{100}{1} + \\binom{100}{2} - \\dots + \\binom{100}{100} = \\boxed{0}.\\]",
    "is_correct": true
  },
